# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# The app already ships a CSP via <meta http-equiv> in _document.tsx, but a meta
# CSP cannot carry frame-ancestors — the spec requires that directive to be
# ignored when delivered that way. So without these headers safe.iotex.io is
# framable by any origin, and a wallet UI whose primary controls are
# "Confirm" / "Execute" is a worthwhile clickjacking target.
#
# X-Frame-Options mirrors what app.safe.global serves; the CSP header carries
# only frame-ancestors, so it constrains framing and nothing else (each policy
# is enforced independently, and a policy that declares no fetch directives
# restricts no fetches).
#
# Deliberately no Permissions-Policy. Safe Apps run in cross-origin iframes and
# receive features through the `allow` attribute built from src/components/
# safe-apps/types.ts, which includes camera, microphone, geolocation and payment.
# Permissions Policy delegation requires the *top-level* document to have the
# feature enabled first, so a blanket `camera=()` here would silently override
# that grant and break capable apps (QR scanners among them) no matter what the
# iframe allows.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
